  "query_preview": "..\n\n- A moderate winter system is on the way for Thursday that is\n  starting to cause increasing concerns for travel. \n\n- Another moderate system is on the way for Friday night and\n  Saturday that will likely cause moderate travel impacts in the\n  region this weekend. \n\nWe've been watching models bring a decaying atmospheric river into\nthe region for Thursday for a while now. The conceptual model of \nhow these systems usually work is that snow levels rise once the \nprecipitation arrives. Most members of the NBM are showing that, \nbut a significant fraction show snow levels dropping down to the \nvalley floors. There have been significant changes in the NBM \nsince last winter, and the latest version is mostly untested with \nwinter weather in our region. The new short term REFS ensemble is \nvery confident in rain in the valleys of western Montana, but it's\nbeen missing the cold pools that are already in place, and it's \nalso untested in the winter. So a lot of uncertainty remains in \nthe precipitation type in the valleys headed into Thursday. \nThere's also considerable differences in the model systems in the \nmountains with the REFS showing around 5 inches of snow at Lolo \nPass by Friday morning, and the NBM giving only 20-30% chances of \n2 inches! It's not unusual for there to be a lot of uncertainty \naround snow levels this time of year, but it is compounded by 2 of\nour best modeling tools being new versions this winter. All that \nsaid, there is high confidence in precipitation, it's going to be \ncloudy and wet on Thanksgiving and Friday morning. The uncertainty\nin the snow levels just means if you're traveling, you should be \nprepared for snowy or icy travel conditions. For central Idaho \naround Grangeville and up to Pierce, those areas will be directly \nexposed to the incoming warmer air and we have higher confidence \nof high snow levels at or above 5,000 ft there. \n\nMost of the uncertainty over the weekend is due to not yet knowing\nhow far south the low pressure system will track, but the\nsituation is improving there. Models have been coalescing towards\na more southerly track, which means more snow and less rain for\nmost of the region. Road crews have been reporting freezing roads\nas low as 4,000ft elevation in western Montana, and with the track\nof that low pressure it indicates lower elevation snow is likely\nthis weekend, especially in west-central and southwest Montana. If\nthe forecast holds, it's likely that we'll be issuing some winter\nweather advisories for the weekend across western Montana. \n\nLemhi county, ID will see more varied conditions with both\nsystems. Snow amounts will strongly depend on elevation. High\nelevation, remote locations like Williams Cr. Summit will see\nseveral inches of snow now through the weekend whereas none is\nexpected in Salmon. \n\nLooking out into early next week, the pattern remains either\nsouthwest flow or a trough. So we expect unsettled weather to\ncontinue with snow showers, but there isn't a strong system\nlooming on the horizon that we're concerned about.",
